1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:01,509
Ten green checks.

2
00:00:01,509 --> 00:00:03,201
One bug was still there.

3
00:00:03,201 --> 00:00:07,513
On the old version of Click, rendering help for a custom option
crashed.

4
00:00:08,208 --> 00:00:11,858
We ran Click’s own regression on the fix and its parent.

5
00:00:11,858 --> 00:00:15,002
Both runs ended with the same passing checks.

6
00:00:15,002 --> 00:00:19,610
Then we staged a completion claim and asked Plum about the original
regression.

7
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:20,860
Before the fix?

8
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:22,956
24.8 percent.

9
00:00:22,956 --> 00:00:24,219
After the fix?

10
00:00:24,219 --> 00:00:26,429
96.5 percent.

11
00:00:26,429 --> 00:00:29,680
Each baseline gave the same score for both.

12
00:00:30,375 --> 00:00:32,661
Here’s a recorded Django agent run.

13
00:00:32,661 --> 00:00:34,883
The original regression fails.

14
00:00:34,883 --> 00:00:38,886
The agent edits the test, sees it pass, and submits.

15
00:00:38,886 --> 00:00:42,585
SWE-bench marked the patch unresolved.

16
00:00:42,585 --> 00:00:44,807
We mapped selected events by hand.

17
00:00:44,807 --> 00:00:47,958
To Plum, the earlier failure still counts.

18
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:50,978
Plum reads each event once.

19
00:00:50,978 --> 00:00:55,069
It carries 48 state values across four requirements.

20
00:00:55,069 --> 00:01:00,258
A recurrent network with 660 learned parameters weighs the checks.

21
00:01:00,258 --> 00:01:02,424
It runs right inside the browser.

22
00:01:03,125 --> 00:01:05,562
Avadhanam inspired the training.

23
00:01:05,562 --> 00:01:11,825
We interleaved evidence, added distractions, and asked about each
requirement along the way.

24
00:01:11,825 --> 00:01:15,120
The simulator supplied the correct probabilities.

25
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,488
The model learned which checks to combine.

26
00:01:18,208 --> 00:01:23,095
In this separate simulation, a new revision clears old evidence.

27
00:01:23,095 --> 00:01:25,431
Plum returns to 50 percent.

28
00:01:25,431 --> 00:01:29,535
A fresh full check passes, and it rises to 90.

29
00:01:29,535 --> 00:01:32,824
A follow-up spot check brings it to 96.

30
00:01:33,542 --> 00:01:38,176
In our held-out simulation, Plum’s Brier error was point one one
eight.

31
00:01:38,176 --> 00:01:41,737
The eight-event baseline scored point two one one.

32
00:01:41,737 --> 00:01:47,229
We tested 256 streams, with three out of four events being
distractors.

33
00:01:47,229 --> 00:01:49,319
The checks had known reliability.

34
00:01:50,042 --> 00:01:51,191
Try it yourself.

35
00:01:51,191 --> 00:01:55,257
Run pip install plum-sbm, then plum demo.

36
00:01:55,257 --> 00:01:58,742
The package includes the weights and runs offline.

37
00:01:58,742 --> 00:02:03,559
Open the web demo to replay the Django run, or feed Plum your own
events.
